/*  chainTruthT -- A GENERATED RULE MUST FAIL WHEN A REQUIRED TERM FAILS.

    BORN RED, on purpose, 2026-09-20. Rows 2, 3 and 4 are the defect: today a
    generated rule succeeds whenever its FIRST term matches, and every later
    required term can fail without failing the rule.

    THE RULING THIS RESTORES IS (c'), 09-09: parseRule reads truthOf(result) and
    nothing else. Generate.rtn:229 tests PRESENCE again -- `if result sukcess =
    true;` -- and a kant body spelling `false` hands back falseResult, which is a
    real node. Non-null. So a short-circuited chain reads as success.

    ⚠ ROW 7 IS A HAZARD PROBE, NOT A REGRESSION ROW. Terms return their LABEL on
    success and truthOf reads a numeric node BY ITS VALUE, so a label holding 0
    could read false and a CORRECT match could fail its chain. It is pinned to
    MATCH. If it goes red when rows 2-4 go green, the fix has traded one defect
    for another and a term inside a chain should return trueResult, leaving the
    label to the label channel.

    ⚠ EVERY DRIVE IS INSIDE ONE ACTION. A drive of a generated parse leaves the
    FILE parse unable to match identifier-initial statements (F-94), so seven
    file-scope drives would silently stop after the first.

    THE OBSERVABLE IS `fireLabel IN  <rule>` under traceParse for rows 1-6,
    validated at BOTH ends before it was trusted: 1 on "search list;" and 0 on
    "nonsense". ⚠ IT DOES NOT WORK FOR A RULE WITH NO ACTION -- fireLabelMethod's
    fire branch needs stuff.actionMethod, and a rule with no code body and no
    aCTion<name> extern has none. wzNum therefore CARRIES A BODY, and row 7's
    observable is that body's own marker, which is the most direct reading there is.

    ROWS -- Search is `search- followedBy GrouP+ SemI-`
      1  "search list;"   MATCH    all four terms present
      2  "search list"    no match SemI is required and absent
      3  "search ;"       no match GrouP+ is required and absent
      4  "search"         no match three required terms absent
      5  "nonsense"       no match first term fails
      6  "xearch list;"   no match first term fails on one character
      7  wzNum("0")       MATCH    the truthOf-reads-a-zero-label hazard probe

    TWO MORE, ADDED 2026-09-20 WHEN TONY RULED THAT A RULE CARRIES NO argument.
    They exist to keep the slot from coming back unnoticed, and each fails a
    different way if it does:
      8  a CALL statement at FILE SCOPE after a drive must RUN. While the slot
         existed, a drive left the file parse unable to match anything
         identifier-initial, so this statement silently never ran -- at exit 0.
         ⚠ A cerr would NOT do: keyword-initial statements parsed throughout.
      9  a RE-EMIT after a drive must read `first() && nameSet()` and must NOT
         contain `argument()`. The slot was a TERM as well as a frame member, so
         a returning slot shows up in the emitted body before it shows up
         anywhere else.
*/
Start();
include("IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser");
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Utilities list;
define
    wzNum isRule NumbeR code={ cerr "WZNUM-FIRED":; };
    wzRun code={
        cerr "CT-ROW1":;
        Search("search list;");
        cerr "CT-ROW2":;
        Search("search list");
        cerr "CT-ROW3":;
        Search("search ;");
        cerr "CT-ROW4":;
        Search("search");
        cerr "CT-ROW5":;
        Search("nonsense");
        cerr "CT-ROW6":;
        Search("xearch list;");
        cerr "CT-ROW7":;
        wzNum("0");
        cerr "CT-ROWEND":;
        };
    ctReEmit code={
        NamE("foo");
        cerr "CT-REEMIT":;
        generateParse(NamE);
        };
    ctAfterDrive code={ cerr "CT-CALL-RAN":; };
    ;
parser(Search);
parser(wzNum);
traceParse(0);
wzRun();
ctReEmit();
ctAfterDrive();
cerr "CHAINTRUTH SENTINEL":;
stop();

